    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d67">

                <pb facs="0163" n="61" xml:id="pg61"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 Steering Committee/506</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Delegate</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 121</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-01">August 1,
                        1935</date>.<lb/>[Received August 12.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my telegram No. 1045 of June
                    19, 2 p.m., with regard to a meeting of the Bureau of the Disarmament Conference
                    in September, I had a conversation on this subject yesterday with Mr. Aghnides,
                    Director of the Disarmament Section of the League of Nations. Mr. Aghnides had
                    just returned from a trip to London where he had discussed this question with
                    Mr. Henderson, President of the Conference. The latter, it appears, is even more
                    determined than ever that the Bureau shall meet in September. To this end Mr.
                    Henderson has discussed with Mr. Aghnides the question of issuing shortly a
                    notice of probable convocation during the meeting of the Assembly, on or about
                    September 13th, formal notice of convocation to be issued shortly after the
                    “warning” notice. The preliminary circular would indicate that in the
                    President’s opinion the Bureau should meet in September in view of all that has
                    taken place since the last Bureau meeting and for the purpose of reviewing the
                    situation and determining upon the future course of the Conference. Mr. Aghnides
                    feels that there is every probability that the meeting of the Bureau will take
                    place at the time indicated since he discussed the question with the French and
                    the British informally during his recent trip and found that they were in accord
                    with Mr. Henderson’s desires.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hugh R. Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
